Z||000022||firstfiero||10-04-2001||09:03 AM||||     general manager of a dominos. I tried the factory thing for a long time and hated it! I have to have human interaction while working. I make about 45k a year depending on bonuses. Not bad to make pizzas.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I didn't realize how much I loved restraunt managment until I worked at a factory for a while.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||bHooper||10-04-2001||09:09 AM||||i just wish i had a job!  until april, i had been in textile manufacturing and sales for 8 or 9 years.  i enjoyed what i did.  i got to travel some, meet lots of different people and basically everyone was the boss (we were a small company).<P>since then, i have gone back to school to gain several networking certifications. <P>unfortunatly, the job market is crap (around here) and i may have to soon take a job at the Adult Video store or Home Depot.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Fformula88||10-04-2001||09:52 AM||||There's gotta be some good perks to running a pizza place!!  Lotsa Pizza!  YUMMMY!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I can relate to that bad job market too!  The economic boom the past 8 years or so has missed Buffalo, along with all of upstate NY for the most part.  There hasn't been any growth!  Most of the jobs out there pay $8-$10 an hour... not exactly good wages to try and live on, and even those jobs are hard to get!  I keep hearing the Carolinas are the place to go.  It surprises me to hear the market isn't too good down their either!<P>Maybe its not SOO bad here, I mean I am pulling in 30K which is enough they aren't threatening to fire me.  But I still don't want to be here for my whole career!||||reg||1
Z||000025||MinnGreenGT||10-04-2001||11:31 AM||||I'm in a pretty good position.  I work as a Graphic Designer for a <A HREF="http://www.xtralite.com" TARGET=_blank>portable tradeshow display company</A> and have been here for about 3 years.  I basically play with graphic programs producing wide-format banners on an big inket printer.<P>I also recently started my own company (with 3 other former co-workers) <A HREF="http://www.romatekgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>Romatek Graphics</A>.  We do graphic design for about anything that comes up- as well as specializing in wide-format printing for the tradeshow industry as well as many other markets including art reproduction!  <P>It's tough working full time (45+ hours) at the first job, then spending my evenings at the new company.  As soon as <A HREF="http://www.romatekgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>Romatek</A> begins to takes off (pay the bills), I'll quit the display company and just do my own thing!<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Raydar||10-04-2001||11:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Same industry, slightly different focus.  Raydar, just wait until someone with a backhoe who didn't call DigSafe chomps through one of your fiber conduits.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Larry,<P>Since I've been in this department (last November), we've only had one "backhoe fade".<BR>Fortunately, we have some redundancy built in. Diverse paths and such.<P>Raydar<P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||bHooper||10-04-2001||12:57 PM||||no the carolina's definatly suck right now.  all the textile mnfg has gone belly up and everyone has gone to tech school.<P>add to that the weak tech industry and boom! no jobs.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||DrRodey||10-04-2001||03:35 PM||||I absolutely LOVE what I do.  Get to help people out when they need it the most, really make a difference in someones life EVERY DAY.  Yeah it's a lot of stress  and you see some things you never wanted to in a million years but there is no feeling better then Pulling up to a house giving it you're all and end up saving not only your patients life but also brightening the day of everyone present.  It might just be the respect that ya get from everyone no matter where ya go, or when past patients/family come up to you on the street for no apparant reason and thank you for doing just what you are required no matter what the outcome of the call was.  I'd say if you want a job that is fun, exciting, energising, interesting, and most of all meaningful.. You have to get into Emergency medicine.  Being an EMT/Paramedic is The best Experience someone can ever have.  That and getting paid to sleep helps.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Formula88||10-04-2001||04:52 PM||||I'm a Computer Analyst with CompuCom systems and I absolutely love my job.  Not as great as NASA, but then, what is?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Basically, I come in and play with the computers for a while, the go home.  It has it's share of headaches, but what job doesn't.  <P>BTW, anyone looking to get on in the I.T. industry, check out CompuCom.  We are growing even now.  It's really a great company.  And if you tell them I referred you, I get a cash bonus!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ciao~||||reg||14
